  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["U22"],
      "pins": ["U22.4", "U22.2", "U22.1"],
      "nets": ["+3V3", "GND", "ESTOP_SINK1"],
      "observed": "U22 is a non-inverting buffer with /OE. Its output pin 4 (Y) is tied directly to the +3V3 rail, while its input pin 2 (A) is tied to GND. Its /OE pin 1 is connected to ESTOP_SINK1. When ESTOP_SINK1 goes low (active e-stop), U22 is enabled and drives its output low, shorting the +3V3 rail to GND through the buffer.",
      "expected": "U22 should be part of the e-stop sink chain (e.g., its output should drive /ESTOP_SINK_ALL or similar), not be tied to a supply rail. Its input should not be tied to GND if its output is tied to +3V3.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U22\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Y\") (pintype \"output\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U22\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"input\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"1\") (name \"+3V3\") ... (node (ref \"U22\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Y\") (pintype \"output\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "When an e-stop is asserted on ESTOP_SINK1, the +3V3 rail is shorted to GND, likely causing catastrophic current draw, power rail collapse, and failure of U22 or the 3.3V regulator. The ESTOP_SINK1 input otherwise has no effect on the e-stop chain.",
      "confidence": 0.98
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R36 has schematic value \"24k\" but its MPN \"FRC0805J240TSD\" and description \"24Ω\" both indicate a 24Ω part. R36 is connected directly across the PoE-derived high-voltage rail between VOUTP and VOUTN.",
      "expected": "R36 should be a 24kΩ bleeder resistor as indicated by its value and the SPICE deck. A 24Ω part across the PoE rail would draw excessive current and dissipate far beyond its 125mW rating.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spice",
          "quote": "R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"218\") (name \"VOUTN\") ... (node (ref \"R36\") (pin \"1\")) ... (net (code \"219\") (name \"VOUTP\") ... (node (ref \"R36\") (pin \"2\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the board is assembled using the specified MPN, R36 will be 24Ω instead of 24kΩ. Across the PoE output this creates a near-short, drawing very large current and likely destroying R36 and damaging the PoE power path. The board will not behave as intended.",
      "confidence": 0.92
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.5", "U11.5", "U10.8", "U11.8"],
      "nets": ["GND", "5V_switched_CAN"],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have their isolated-side ground pins (GND2, pin 5) tied directly to the main board GND net. Their isolated-side supply pins (VCC2, pin 8) are tied to 5V_switched_CAN, which is a non-isolated 5V rail derived from the main +5V rail.",
      "expected": "For the two isolated CAN buses described in the specification, the isolated-side ground (GND2) and supply (VCC2) must be galvanically isolated from the logic ground and logic supply, using a separate isolated ground and isolated power source.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"52\") (name \"5V_switched_CAN\") ... (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"8\") (pinfunction \"VCC2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"8\") (pinfunction \"VCC2\") (pintype \"power_in\")))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation barrier is completely bypassed. The CAN buses are not isolated as specified, defeating the purpose of the isolated transceivers and allowing ground loops or common-mode voltages between the rover's logic ground and the motor controllers.",
      "confidence": 0.88
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain multiple serious anomalies. The most critical is U22, whose output is tied to +3V3 while its input is tied to GND, causing a +3V3-to-GND short when ESTOP_SINK1 is asserted. Additionally, R36 is specified as 24k but its MPN/description indicate 24Ω, which would place a near-short across the PoE rail. Also, the isolated CAN transceivers U10/U11 have their isolated grounds and supplies tied to the non-isolated GND and 5V rails, defeating the required isolation."
}

